Aim: System Calls Demonstration – Write and execute basic system calls (fork(), exec(), wait(), exit()) in Linux.

Theory:
1. What is a System Call?
 A system call is a mechanism through which a user program requests services from the operating system. It allows programs to interact with the kernel to perform operations such as creating processes, executing programs, and terminating processes.

2. Types of System Calls Used
1. fork()
•	The fork() system call creates a new process called the child process.
•	The process that calls fork() is called the parent process.
•	Both processes execute independently after the process is created.
2. exec()
•	The exec() family of system calls is used to execute a new program.
•	It replaces the current process's program with a new program.
•	Example: execl() can execute the Linux ls command.
3. wait()
•	The wait() system call makes the parent process wait until a child process terminates.
•	It helps synchronize the execution of parent and child processes.
4. exit()
•	The exit() function terminates the calling process.
•	It returns an exit status to the operating system.

Code:
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main()
{
    int pid = fork();

    if (pid == 0)
    {
        printf("Child Process\n");

        execl("/bin/ls", "ls", NULL);

        exit(0);
    }
    else
    {
        wait(NULL);
        printf("Parent Process\n");
        exit(0);
    }

    return 0;
}

